from (values
('127.392.d.5.A',3,'Direct current (DC) flows:','["only in water", "in one direction only", "back and forth", "in circles only"]'::jsonb,1,'DC does not reverse direction.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.A',4,'Which source produces DC?','["A household generator", "A power-line transformer", "A battery", "A wall outlet"]'::jsonb,2,'Batteries supply steady DC.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.B',3,'In an atom, electrons carry a:','["no charge", "positive charge", "double charge", "negative charge"]'::jsonb,3,'Electrons are negatively charged.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.B',4,'Materials with loosely bound outer electrons make good:','["conductors", "filters", "dividers", "insulators"]'::jsonb,0,'Free electrons carry current easily.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.C',3,'A zigzag line on a schematic usually represents a:','["battery", "resistor", "capacitor", "switch"]'::jsonb,1,'The zigzag is the resistor symbol.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.C',4,'On a schematic, a line that crosses another with a dot at the crossing indicates the wires are:','["insulated", "separate", "connected", "broken"]'::jsonb,2,'A junction dot means electrical connection.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.D',3,'A switch in a circuit is used to:','["produce light", "store charge", "raise voltage", "open or close the current path"]'::jsonb,3,'Switches control current flow.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.D',4,'In a circuit, the ground is a:','["common reference point for voltage", "type of resistor", "source of current", "fuse"]'::jsonb,0,'Ground is the 0 V reference.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.E',3,'A resistor with bands brown, black, red has a value of:','["100 ohms", "1,000 ohms", "10,000 ohms", "10 ohms"]'::jsonb,1,'Brown=1, black=0, red=x100, so 1,000 ohms.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.E',4,'A resistor''s tolerance band that is gold means a tolerance of:','["20 percent", "10 percent", "5 percent", "1 percent"]'::jsonb,2,'Gold is 5%; silver is 10%.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.F',3,'Ohm''s Law can be written V = I x R. Solving for current gives:','["I = R / V", "I = V + R", "I = V x R", "I = V / R"]'::jsonb,3,'Divide both sides by R.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.F',4,'A 12 V source drives current through a 4 ohm resistor. What is the current?','["3 amps", "0.33 amps", "48 amps", "8 amps"]'::jsonb,0,'I = 12 / 4 = 3 A.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.G',4,'A device draws 2 amps from a 120 volt outlet. Its power is:','["122 watts", "240 watts", "118 watts", "60 watts"]'::jsonb,1,'P = 120 x 2 = 240 W.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.H',3,'In a series circuit, the current is the same:','["only in the last part", "only in the first part", "through every component", "in none of the parts"]'::jsonb,2,'Series elements share one path.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.H',4,'In a parallel circuit, the voltage across each branch is:','["zero", "divided equally", "different for each", "the same"]'::jsonb,3,'Parallel branches connect to the same two nodes.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.I',3,'A 100 ohm resistor carries 0.2 amps. The voltage across it is:','["20 volts", "100.2 volts", "0.002 volts", "500 volts"]'::jsonb,0,'V = 0.2 x 100 = 20 V.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.I',4,'Two 10 ohm resistors in series are connected to a 20 V source. The current is:','["4 amps", "1 amp", "0.5 amps", "2 amps"]'::jsonb,1,'Total R = 20 ohms, so I = 1 A.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.J',3,'A 5 ohm resistor carries 2 amps. The power dissipated is:','["7 watts", "10 watts", "20 watts", "50 watts"]'::jsonb,2,'P = I^2 x R = 4 x 5 = 20 W.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.J',4,'A 60 watt bulb runs on 120 volts. The current is:','["7200 amps", "60 amps", "2 amps", "0.5 amps"]'::jsonb,3,'I = P / V = 60 / 120 = 0.5 A.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.K',3,'A current of 0.005 amps written in scientific notation is:','["5 x 10^-3 amps", "5 x 10^-2 amps", "5 x 10^3 amps", "0.5 x 10^-3 amps"]'::jsonb,0,'0.005 = 5 x 10^-3.','original'),
('127.392.d.5.K',4,'A resistance of 4,700 ohms is written in engineering notation as:','["4.7 megohms", "4.7 kilohms", "47 kilohms", "0.47 kilohms"]'::jsonb,1,'4,700 = 4.7 x 10^3 = 4.7 k.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.A',3,'In alternating current (AC), the direction of flow:','["stops permanently", "moves in one direction", "reverses periodically", "never changes"]'::jsonb,2,'AC alternates direction.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.A',4,'Household electricity in the United States is AC with a frequency of:','["120 hertz", "6 hertz", "50 hertz", "60 hertz"]'::jsonb,3,'U.S. power is 60 Hz.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.B',3,'A sine wave has a peak voltage of 10 V. Its peak-to-peak voltage is:','["20 volts", "5 volts", "10 volts", "14.1 volts"]'::jsonb,0,'Peak-to-peak is twice the peak.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.B',4,'The RMS value of a sine wave with a 100 V peak is about:','["141 volts", "70.7 volts", "63.7 volts", "200 volts"]'::jsonb,1,'RMS = 0.707 x peak.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.C',3,'When more mechanical load is placed on a generator, the current it supplies generally:','["falls to zero", "decreases", "increases", "stays unchanged"]'::jsonb,2,'More load means more current drawn and more torque required.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.C',4,'A generator turning harder when you plug in more devices shows that more electrical load requires more:','["insulation", "silence", "sunlight", "mechanical input power"]'::jsonb,3,'Output power must be supplied by the shaft.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.D',3,'A transformer is used to:','["step AC voltage up or down", "create current", "store energy", "convert AC to DC"]'::jsonb,0,'Transformers change AC voltage levels.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.D',4,'A transformer works only with:','["chemical energy", "alternating current", "static", "direct current"]'::jsonb,1,'A changing field is needed to induce voltage.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.E',3,'A transformer has 100 primary turns and 200 secondary turns. With 10 V on the primary, the secondary voltage is:','["10 volts", "200 volts", "20 volts", "5 volts"]'::jsonb,2,'Voltage scales with the turns ratio (2:1).','original'),
('127.392.d.6.E',4,'A step-down transformer has a 10:1 turns ratio. If the primary is 120 V, the secondary is:','["10 volts", "1,200 volts", "110 volts", "12 volts"]'::jsonb,3,'120 / 10 = 12 V.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.F',3,'Capacitance is measured in units called:','["farads", "henrys", "watts", "ohms"]'::jsonb,0,'The farad is the unit of capacitance.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.F',4,'As the frequency of an AC signal increases, capacitive reactance:','["becomes infinite", "decreases", "is unchanged", "increases"]'::jsonb,1,'Xc = 1 / (2 pi f C).','original'),
('127.392.d.6.G',3,'Inductance is measured in units called:','["ohms", "farads", "henrys", "coulombs"]'::jsonb,2,'The henry is the unit of inductance.','original'),
('127.392.d.6.G',4,'When the frequency of an AC signal goes up, inductive reactance:','["is unchanged", "becomes zero", "falls", "rises"]'::jsonb,3,'XL = 2 pi f L.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.A',3,'Kirchhoff''s voltage law states that the sum of voltages around a closed loop is:','["zero", "one", "equal to current", "infinite"]'::jsonb,0,'Voltage rises equal voltage drops around a loop.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.A',4,'Kirchhoff''s current law states that the current entering a junction equals the current:','["lost in it", "leaving it", "stored in it", "created in it"]'::jsonb,1,'Charge is conserved at a node.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.B',3,'To build a parallel circuit on a breadboard, components are connected so that:','["only one is connected", "none touch the source", "each has its own path across the source", "all share one path"]'::jsonb,2,'Parallel branches each connect across the supply.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.B',4,'Before powering up a circuit you have built, you should first:','["connect it to mains", "remove the resistors", "skip the check", "check connections against the schematic"]'::jsonb,3,'Verification prevents damage.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.C',3,'Moving a magnet through a coil of wire induces a:','["voltage", "mass", "sound only", "fuel"]'::jsonb,0,'Electromagnetic induction creates voltage.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.C',4,'Faraday''s law says that the induced voltage in a coil depends on how fast the:','["wire''s color changes", "magnetic field changes", "coil is named", "resistance is painted"]'::jsonb,1,'Induced emf is proportional to the rate of flux change.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.D',3,'A good first step in troubleshooting a dead circuit is to:','["guess", "throw it away", "check the power source", "replace every part"]'::jsonb,2,'Start with the simplest, most common cause.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.D',4,'Dividing a faulty circuit into sections and testing each is called:','["guess-and-replace", "random testing", "brute-force wiring", "half-split troubleshooting"]'::jsonb,3,'Splitting narrows down the fault quickly.','original'),
