('127.392.d.7.E',4,'A component marked with a band at one end that allows current in one direction only is a:','["capacitor", "diode", "inductor", "resistor"]'::jsonb,1,'The band marks the diode''s cathode.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.F',3,'Torque in a DC motor is produced when current in the armature interacts with a:','["light beam", "water", "magnetic field", "gravity field"]'::jsonb,2,'Current in a magnetic field feels a force.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.F',4,'Increasing the current through a motor''s armature generally makes its torque:','["decrease", "reverse", "vanish", "increase"]'::jsonb,3,'Torque is proportional to armature current.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.G',3,'Counter electromotive force (CEMF) in a motor is produced because:','["the spinning armature acts like a generator", "the motor is too cold", "the housing is plastic", "the brushes are painted"]'::jsonb,0,'Motion in a field induces an opposing voltage.','original'),
('127.392.d.7.G',4,'As a motor speeds up, its CEMF increases, which causes the armature current to:','["double", "decrease", "increase", "remain at a maximum"]'::jsonb,1,'Higher CEMF reduces net voltage across the armature.','original'),
